### Additional Context

### MongoDB Documentation

- [Watch a Database](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/changeStreams/#watch-a-database)
- [Watch a Deployment](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/changeStreams/#watch-a-deployment)

### Performance Considerations

**Database-Level Watching**:
- ✅ Single change stream instead of N (one per collection)
- ✅ Automatically picks up new collections
- ⚠️ All events go through single stream (potential bottleneck for very high volume)
- **Recommended for**: Databases with < 50 collections

**Collection-Level Watching**:
- ✅ Parallelism across collections
- ✅ Can apply different batching/retry settings per collection
- ⚠️ Must update config when adding collections
- **Recommended for**: Large databases with known, stable collections

**Deployment-Level Watching**:
- ✅ Single stream for entire cluster
- ⚠️ Very high volume, potential bottleneck
- ⚠️ Requires cluster-wide permissions
- **Recommended for**: Monitoring/audit use cases only

### Breaking Changes

**Changed Default Behavior**:
- Old default: Empty collections list → watches nothing
- New default: `WatchLevel::Database` → watches entire database

This is a **minor breaking change** for users relying on the default. Migration is simple:

```rust
// If you want old behavior (watch nothing), explicitly specify:
.watch_collections(vec![])

// Or disable the pipeline
```

### Implementation Checklist

- [ ] Add `WatchLevel` enum
- [ ] Update `PipelineConfig` to use `watch_level` instead of `collections`
- [ ] Deprecate old `collections()` method (keep functional)
- [ ] Implement database-level watching with `db.watch()`
- [ ] Implement deployment-level watching with `client.watch()`
- [ ] Handle resume tokens correctly for each watch level
- [ ] Update all examples to show new API
- [ ] Add tests for each watch level
- [ ] Update documentation (README, getting-started, module docs)
- [ ] Add migration guide in CHANGELOG

